WEBVTT

STYLE
::cue(c.color9CD5FF) { color: #9CD5FF; }

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.000 line:5% align:center
<b><c.color9CD5FF>One Pace Addon</c></b>

2
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.840
<b>Noland the Liar</b>

3
00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:58.360
I can't believe our luck.

4
00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.450
Seriously.

5
00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.430
That orangutan almost sunk us.

6
00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.160
And now it's up to me to fix it
all and keep this thing afloat.

7
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.870
Seems to me
that this busted old ship

8
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.160
is on her last leg.

9
00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:09.210
We need a new one.

10
00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.710
How dare you even consider
replacing this boat?

11
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:12.700
Yeah, how dare you!

12
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.530
We all know the story, Usopp.

13
00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:16.870
And try not to be so defensive.

14
00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.080
The Going Merry is an
important part of our crew,

15
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.460
and we all know we have
to take care of her.

16
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.630
You shouldn't take
everything so personally.

17
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.920
You're right. I'm sorry.

18
00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:28.880
Oops!

19
00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:31.550
What are you doing?!

20
00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:33.930
We left Mock Town
and headed east

21
00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.560
by circling around the
northern shore of the island.

22
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.680
Our destination is here.

23
00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.230
We're supposed
to meet that man there.

24
00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:40.970
What's his name?

25
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.900
His name is Mont Blanc Cricket.

26
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:43.730
And you're sure that

27
00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.610
he'll be able to
help us out, right?

28
00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.770
Whoa! Amazing!

29
00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.350
That's where he lives?

30
00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:52.940
It's a freakin' palace!

31
00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.270
I've never seen
a house that big before.

32
00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.980
This Mont Blanc Cricket
guy must be really loaded.

33
00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.780
Ugh. Look closer.

34
00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.650
It's really unattractive.

35
00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.950
A guy's gotta have some serious
guts to build a house that ugly.

36
00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.410
Hmm? What's that?

37
00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.620
This isn't a palace at all!

38
00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:14.620
– What?
– What?

39
00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.750
The actual house
is only half that size.

40
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:17.970
What you see is a facade.

41
00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.010
A fake.

42
00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:20.830
This guy's even
more vain than I thought.

43
00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.330
Why exactly did Cricket
decide to leave his home

44
00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:24.600
and come live here?

45
00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.640
I don't know all the details.

46
00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:28.960
But I think it has something
to do with his belief

47
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.760
that a large amount
of gold rests somewhere

48
00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:31.880
in the hills of Jaya.

49
00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:32.620
G‐Gold?

50
00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:33.760
Do you think he's here searching

51
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.070
for some sort of
buried treasure?

52
00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:37.300
Well, I just don't know.

53
00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.060
Gold!

54
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.480
Try over here, Chopper.

55
00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:42.530
Dig up this spot.

56
00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.410
I'll be right there, Nami.

57
00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.870
It must be hard to live
alone in such a remote area.

58
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:48.000
Hello!

59
00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:49.900
Don't just barge in there.

60
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.920
Huh. Looks like nobody's home.

61
00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.090
Anybody here?

62
00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.690
I guess he's gone.

63
00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:56.700
Get out of there, you idiot.

64
00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.750
This guy could be dangerous.

65
00:03:58.770 --> 00:03:59.850
Hey, you guys.

66
00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:01.030
Nobody's home.

67
00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:04.630
Huh... What's this?

68
00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.110
A book.

69
00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:09.310
And it looks pretty old.

70
00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.660
It's called "Noland, the Liar."

71
00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.910
Huh. Sounds like
it could be good.

72
00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.830
I sure like the title.

73
00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.500
"Noland, the Liar"?

74
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:19.750
Yeah, have you heard of it?

75
00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:20.920
It says here,

76
00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:23.300
it was originally published
in the North Blue territory.

77
00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:24.250
It was!

78
00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:25.970
I was born in the
North Blue territory.

79
00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.350
I'm certain I must
have told you guys that.

80
00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:29.680
No, that's news to me.

81
00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.520
I always thought that
you were born in the East.

82
00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:32.290
I grew up there.

83
00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.570
Enough already, Chopper.

84
00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:34.650
What are you doing?

85
00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.200
Where I come from,
this is a famous story.

86
00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:42.460
It's a fairy tale supposedly
based on the life of Noland,

87
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.510
who lived in the
North Blue a long time ago.

88
00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:51.100
This is a story
of ancient origins.

89
00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:55.810
The events herein transpired
more than 400 years ago.

90
00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:59.060
There once was a man by
the name of Mont Blanc Noland

91
00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:01.920
who lived in the
North Blue countryside.

92
00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:05.720
Noland, who was an explorer,
always regaled the crowds

93
00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.070
with stories of his
incredible exploits.

94
00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.180
But nobody knew whether or not

95
00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:12.600
to take Noland's
tall tales seriously.

96
00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:15.850
One day, upon returning
from yet another adventure,

97
00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.690
he reported to his king
all that he had seen.

98
00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.650
"I saw a city of gold on
an island in the great sea,"

99
00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:22.850
declared Noland.

100
00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.230
And the courageous king decided

101
00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:28.240
that he must have this
treasure for his kingdom.

102
00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:30.500
The king set out with Noland
and two thousand

103
00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:31.990
of his finest soldiers.

104
00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:34.280
Along the way they
endured treacherous storms

105
00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.120
and battled many
ferocious sea creatures.

106
00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.160
At the end of their journey
only the King, Noland,

107
00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.500
and 100 soldiers
survived to set foot

108
00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:44.990
on the island that
housed the city of gold.

109
00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:46.180
And all they found
for their troubles

110
00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:50.100
was a normal island covered
with an ordinary jungle.

111
00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:53.690
And so, upon their return,
Noland was sentenced to death

112
00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:55.730
for the crime of
lying to his King.

113
00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:58.650
His last words were as follows.

114
00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:02.280
"I can only assume that the
city of gold sunk into the sea."

115
00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.240
The King and his
court were dumbfounded.

116
00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.320
Nobody believed Noland anymore.

117
00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:09.280
And the last of his days were
spent in shame and ridicule

118
00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.250
before his sentence
of death was carried out

119
00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.670
before a cheering crowd.

120
00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:14.880
It says here that
the book is based

121
00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:17.840
on the true story
of Noland, the Liar.

122
00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:20.480
<b>Mock Town, Jaya</b>

123
00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.090
Mont Blanc Cricket
is a descendant

124
00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:25.970
of the infamous
Montblanc Noland?

125
00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:28.260
I heard that fairy tale
all the time as a kid.

126
00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:29.900
But I never knew that
Jaya was the island

127
00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:31.950
where he thought
he saw the city of gold.

128
00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.780
When we were growing up
in the North Blue,

129
00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:34.910
we were always warned

130
00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:37.580
that if we lied, we'd suffer
the same fate as Noland

131
00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:40.150
and be ridiculed until we
were eventually put to death.

132
00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:45.030
It makes sense that
Cricket's related to that fool,

133
00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.550
but I can't believe that Jaya is
the island from the fairy tale.

134
00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:51.080
Pardon the intrusion,
but if I heard you correctly,

135
00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.130
you're saying a descendant
of Noland is here,

136
00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.900
400 years after the fact,
looking for that gold?

137
00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:58.220
He's gotta be doing this
to clear his family name

138
00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.790
and regain some
small amount of respect.

139
00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:07.870
This story keeps getting
better and better.

140
00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.800
Can he seriously
hope to wipe away

141
00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:13.130
over 400 years
of embarrassment

142
00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.380
by bringing back
a few pieces of gold?

143
00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:16.970
What a joke!

144
00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:21.210
Sounds like he's a
few bricks short of a wall.

145
00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:31.970
Alas, the Liar died

146
00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:34.890
before he could prove
himself to the people.

147
00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:37.770
All he ever really wanted to do

148
00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.730
was live the life
of an adventurer.

149
00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:41.860
That last part's
not in the book.

150
00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:44.360
Don't try to tack on your
own sentimental nonsense.

151
00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:48.200
Did he just fall in the water?

152
00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.620
What's going on?

153
00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.080
Who the hell are you?

154
00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:53.160
You've got nerve,

155
00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:57.750
<b>Pirate Mont Blanc Cricket
Saruyama Alliance's Last Boss</b>

156
00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:55.660
relaxing at someone's
house without permission.

157
00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:57.750
This is my land,
and I suggest you leave.

158
00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:00.290
Hurry, Usopp. Move it! Go and
fish Luffy out of the sea.

159
00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:01.750
I know why you're here.

160
00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.720
You're after my gold!

161
00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:12.980
Sanji!

162
00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:14.800
Sanji, no!

163
00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:17.260
Don't worry.
This guy's a terrible shot.

164
00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:20.110
Hey! Cut it out!

165
00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:20.850
Idiot.

166
00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.770
Never underestimate your foe.

167
00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.270
Out of the way.
I'll handle this.

168
00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:36.160
You really need to
learn to be more careful.

169
00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:38.080
But I thought I saw...

170
00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.430
...bubbles in the water,

171
00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:43.100
so I looked closer and
then a chestnut appeared.

172
00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:44.390
But it wasn't a chestnut.

173
00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:48.010
It was a guy,
and he pulled me in.

174
00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:50.400
What's with him?

175
00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:54.320
I need more cold towels.

176
00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.600
And open up those windows.

177
00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.480
What's Caisson disease?

178
00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:00.350
So this guy is really sick, huh?

179
00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:01.580
Yeah.

180
00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:04.290
Caisson disease is an illness
that sometimes afflicts divers.

181
00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:05.220
Though it's called a disease,

182
00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.520
it isn't actually
a chronic condition.

183
00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.400
It's caused by a lack
of decompression

184
00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.400
when surfacing from the seabed.

185
00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.820
The nitrogen in the
blood releases too quickly

186
00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.770
and causes gas bubbles to
form in the circulatory system.

187
00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:18.940
And when those
gas bubbles expand

188
00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:21.940
they can cause considerable
damage to muscles and joints

189
00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:24.060
and seriously
decrease blood flow.

190
00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.720
So it's a mystery disease.

191
00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:26.760
Think you can help him?

192
00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:28.030
I'm not sure.

193
00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:30.360
My guess is he kept diving
everyday without allowing time

194
00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:33.640
for his body to recover and
it finally caught up with him.

195
00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:35.270
That's why he passed
out during the fight.

196
00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:37.390
But why in the world
did he do that?

197
00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:38.680
I don't know,

198
00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:40.520
but he was taking a big risk.

199
00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:43.990
In some cases,
Caisson disease can cause death.

200
00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.660
So he's lucky to be alive.

201
00:09:58.460 --> 00:09:59.710
Oh hey, Masira.

202
00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:01.340
I guess you're
still alive after all.

203
00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:02.970
Why do you say that?

204
00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:04.100
Forget about it.

205
00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:06.070
Do you know what all that
gunfire was about earlier?

206
00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:07.070
I'm not sure,

207
00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.400
but that's where I'm
headed now, to find out.

208
00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:10.320
Well, come on then.

209
00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.280
I'm worried about the old‐timer.

210
00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:17.300
Hey, Old‐timer.
Are you all right?

211
00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:20.520
They've come to kill us.

212
00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:22.970
What in the world are
you people doing here?

213
00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:24.980
And just what are
you doing to our friend?

214
00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:25.890
We're trying to help.

215
00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:27.860
So I suggest you
either lend us a hand

216
00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:29.320
or find someone else to bother.

217
00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:30.450
Don't antagonize them, Luffy.

218
00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:31.530
You know what
they're capable of,

219
00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.980
and now there're two of
those savages to deal with.

220
00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:35.100
They're savages!

221
00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:38.100
Everyone, jump out
the windows and run away.

222
00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:40.900
Thank you, guys.

223
00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:41.660
What did they say?

224
00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:44.750
So you guys
live on this island, too?

225
00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:47.990
Yep. This old‐timer's house
is the official headquarters

226
00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:50.120
of the Saruyama
Alliance of Jaya.

227
00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:51.080
We call this home,

228
00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.120
but we spend most of
our time on our ships.

229
00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:54.000
We're more comfortable at sea

230
00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.020
'cause this place
is too small for us.

231
00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:58.480
You guys are
too big for your own good.

232
00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:00.070
But it's all in how
you look at it, I guess.

233
00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:01.460
To a giant you'd still look like

234
00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:02.950
nothing more
than a ball of earwax.

235
00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:05.740
Well, those three sure
are getting along well,

236
00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:06.800
now, aren't they?

237
00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.890
Yeah, seems like they
have a lot in common.

238
00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:09.680
I'm pretty impressed you managed

239
00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:10.910
to get Masira
here off your ship.

240
00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:14.420
It's quite a feat for someone as
small as you to overpower him.

241
00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:15.920
Eh, it wasn't really that hard.

242
00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:17.040
Huh? What's that?

243
00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:19.590
I'm sure you could kick his ass.

244
00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:21.420
Luffy! He's waking up!

245
00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:22.840
He's awake?

246
00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:26.720
He's right!

247
00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:29.260
I can kick Masira's ass!

248
00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:31.760
Hey, chestnut head.

249
00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:34.270
I got a question for ya.

250
00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.480
I'm sorry I attacked you.

251
00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:41.740
I thought you were nothing more
than another group of bandits

252
00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:43.490
who had come to steal my gold.

253
00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:45.500
Did someone say
something about gold?

254
00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:46.450
Just calm down, Nami.

255
00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.810
What exactly was it
you wanted to ask me?

256
00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:51.710
We're searching for sky island
and were told you might be able

257
00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:53.360
to tell us how to get there.

258
00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:55.450
Are you serious?

259
00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:01.990
Don't tell me you really
believe that place exists.

260
00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:03.410
Hey! Stop it.

261
00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:04.620
You can't attack a sick man.

262
00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:06.460
You mean there's no sky island?

263
00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:07.290
Are you sure?

264
00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:12.890
I don't know. See...

265
00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:16.640
Nobody knows if the
sky island really exists or not.

266
00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:17.640
I know of only one man

267
00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:20.310
who claims to have seen
the island for himself.

268
00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:23.610
But he's considered
a liar of epic proportions.

269
00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:24.200
Wah!

270
00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:26.620
What are you looking at?

271
00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:27.660
There's an old fairy tale

272
00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:30.620
that the people of the
North Blue know very well.

273
00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:32.790
It's called "Noland, the Liar."

274
00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:33.830
Wah!

275
00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.520
Stop looking at me, Luffy.
My name's not Noland.

276
00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:37.650
Is he right?

277
00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:40.570
And is this island the one
mentioned in the fairy tale?

278
00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:43.240
Hmph. Yeah,
Noland's my grandfather's,

279
00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:46.990
grandfather's grandfather. He's
a distant ancestor of mine.

280
00:12:47.030 --> 00:12:48.910
I am his descendant.

281
00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:50.160
But we're so far removed,

282
00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:54.200
I doubt there's even a single
drop of his blood in my veins.

283
00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:55.750
The Mont Blanc family
was driven out

284
00:12:55.790 --> 00:12:57.920
of the North Blue country
a long time ago,

285
00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:01.750
and we've managed to keep
a very low profile ever since.

286
00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:04.000
People still denounce
the family name.

287
00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:05.890
But we've never
despised him for this.

288
00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:07.400
But why?

289
00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:09.660
Because Noland,
contrary to popular belief,

290
00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:11.320
was an very honest person.

291
00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:13.620
That's not what
the fairy tale said.

292
00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:15.200
As the story goes,

293
00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:18.370
Noland's last words
were as follows.

294
00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:21.860
"I can only assume that the
city of gold sunk into the sea."

295
00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:22.740
This line is accompanied

296
00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:25.530
by an idiotic face
in the illustration.

297
00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.870
But Noland was no idiot.

298
00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:29.120
He knew what he saw,

299
00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:31.210
and I believe he
wasn't lying about it.

300
00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:33.300
Noland was sure the
island he stumbled upon,

301
00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:35.420
where he found
the ruins of a city of gold,

302
00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.300
was the island of Jaya.

303
00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.850
But when he returned with
the king, there was no gold.

304
00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:43.220
Unsuccessful, he insisted
that the ancient city

305
00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:46.090
had submerged due
to shifts in the earth's crust.

306
00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:47.400
But everyone dismissed this

307
00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:50.650
as the last‐ditch excuse
of a doomed man.

308
00:13:50.690 --> 00:13:53.240
Noland was given
a public execution.

309
00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:55.530
When he died,
so did his discoveries.

310
00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:57.500
Only the legend
of the liar remained.

311
00:13:57.550 --> 00:13:58.500
I get it.

312
00:13:58.560 --> 00:13:59.600
You're trying to find
the ancient city of gold

313
00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:00.680
at the bottom of the sea
in order to finally clear

314
00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:03.650
the Mont Blanc family name
and prove Noland right.

315
00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:04.990
Don't be ridiculous.

316
00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:07.190
Usopp!

317
00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:11.290
I don't really
care if the ancient city of gold

318
00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:12.200
exists or not.

319
00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:13.500
The actions of my ancestor

320
00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:15.800
have nothing to do
with me and my life.

321
00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:18.670
You can't possibly begin to
imagine what it's like as a kid

322
00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:20.630
for everyone to laugh
at you and ridicule you

323
00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:23.180
because you're a descendant
of that idiot, Noland.

324
00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:25.720
That's what I've dealt
with my whole life.

325
00:14:27.890 --> 00:14:30.430
Anyway, over the past 400 years

326
00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:31.680
a great many of my family

327
00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:35.740
have set sail to find the city
of gold, prove Noland right,

328
00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:38.410
and restore the Mont Blanc name.

329
00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:40.320
But each and every one
of them went missing.

330
00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:43.700
I was ashamed to be a member
of such a pathetic family.

331
00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:46.750
So I decided to leave
home become a pirate.

332
00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:49.350
Wow! So you're
a pirate just like us?

333
00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:51.640
I didn't even really
want to become a pirate.

334
00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.730
I just wanted out
of Noland's shadow.

335
00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:55.600
To be my own man.

336
00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:59.180
But over time I came
to assemble my own pirate crew.

337
00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:02.350
Finally, I was seen as a
leader, not a laughingstock.

338
00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:05.400
And I spanned the world
looking for adventures.

339
00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:09.150
Then ten years ago as
my crew and I sailed a calm sea,

340
00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:12.000
we discovered this island
purely by chance.

341
00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:13.050
And ironically,

342
00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:15.680
of all my family members
who sought out this place,

343
00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:19.440
who lost their lives doing so,
it was I who found it.

344
00:15:19.490 --> 00:15:22.410
I who despised
the Mont Blanc name.

345
00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:26.150
We came ashore, but found
no trace of the city of gold.

346
00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:27.480
I stood on a cape of the island

347
00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:30.110
and felt I was
destined to be there.

348
00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:31.940
Let's settle this, Noland.

349
00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:33.660
Once and for all.

350
00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:34.820
You're on your own.

351
00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:36.450
We don't want anything
to do with this.

352
00:15:36.490 --> 00:15:38.120
We're setting sail and leaving.

353
00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:39.370
If you wanna stay here

354
00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:43.540
and search for gold that doesn't
exist, you're doing it alone.

355
00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:45.820
So long, Captain.

356
00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:53.350
I don't care
if I find the city or not.

357
00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:56.060
I'm not here to prove
Noland right or wrong.

358
00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:58.810
I have no interest
in rewriting history.

359
00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:02.570
All I'm doing is following
what I believe is my destiny.

360
00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:06.240
This is between me and Noland.

361
00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:07.820
I'm facing what I fear,

362
00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:12.130
and whatever the outcome,
the act is all that matters.

363
00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:16.500
Wow. What a sad story.

364
00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:19.020
But what about the monkeys.

365
00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:22.430
How did they come to be on
this island with you anyways?

366
00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:24.720
I'm sure that's
another story of bravery

367
00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:26.430
and the face of incredible odds.

368
00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:29.180
Your life reads like a great
book you know, Cricket?

369
00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:31.290
They were just
fans of the fairy tale.

370
00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:32.220
They're fans?

371
00:16:32.690 --> 00:16:34.600
How did they find you?

372
00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:36.730
I think it was
five or six years ago.

373
00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:39.510
They heard a rumor
and set out to find me.

374
00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:42.870
Those two truly believe that the
city of gold exists somewhere.

375
00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:44.960
And they're helping me find it.

376
00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.210
Ahhh!

377
00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:50.440
Shojo, you have no idea
what you just got yourself into!

378
00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:51.570
Bring it on you big ape.

379
00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:52.720
I'm ready when you are.

380
00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:54.720
Just step up and
you'll get knocked down.

381
00:16:54.760 --> 00:16:57.720
The sea surrounding
this island is very deep.

382
00:16:57.720 --> 00:16:59.600
And in those dark, cold depths,

383
00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:02.670
you're surrounded by a
profound sense of solitude.

384
00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:05.650
When I first arrived here,

385
00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:09.410
I spent my time searching
the sea bed alone.

386
00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:10.950
They entered my life
without permission

387
00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:14.290
and assigned themselves
as my crew without asking.

388
00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:16.830
And they do nothing
but fight all the time.

389
00:17:16.830 --> 00:17:19.830
But having these idiots
around puts me at ease.

390
00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:22.850
And truthfully,
I enjoy their company.

391
00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:24.820
You understand?

392
00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:27.340
I understand completely.

393
00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:28.760
True friends lift each other up

394
00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:31.480
and give each other
strength in times of need.

395
00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:34.230
Are you just about finished
telling us your life story?

396
00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:35.860
Shut your mouth
you ungrateful brat.

397
00:17:38.530 --> 00:17:39.620
Stop it.

398
00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:43.180
Look, all I really want to
know about is sky island.

399
00:17:44.430 --> 00:17:46.430
You're a pushy guy, aren't ya?

400
00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:48.140
But like I told you earlier,

401
00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:51.210
the only person who's ever
seen the sky island is Noland.

402
00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.220
And nobody knows
if he was telling the truth

403
00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:55.430
or if it was just
another tall tale.

404
00:17:55.480 --> 00:17:57.770
Is there any record
of his trip to the island?

405
00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:01.190
Officially, no, but he
does mention it in here.

406
00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:02.400
What do you have there?

407
00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:04.570
That isn't
Noland's logbook, is it?

408
00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:05.870
Yeah, it is.

409
00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:08.620
Read from the
bottom of that page.

410
00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:09.660
Amazing.

411
00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:12.570
This logbook is
over 400 years old.

412
00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:14.860
June 21st, year 1120.

413
00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:16.860
A sunny day on the
Sea of Ephemeris.

414
00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:19.200
Upon departing
from the Jolly Villa.

415
00:18:19.300 --> 00:18:21.590
We left the harbor and
sailed into the rising sun

416
00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:23.550
at a heading of
east by northeast

417
00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:25.810
with the wind at out back.

418
00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:29.910
At midday we happened across
a bumboat carrying a rare item.

419
00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:33.460
A single person contraption
called a sky waver.

420
00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:36.460
This small, mysterious vessel
can generate its own wind

421
00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:39.080
and sail at great speeds
even on a still day.

422
00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:41.580
But the ski‐like ship
was not easily maneuvered.

423
00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:42.670
I couldn't control it,

424
00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:45.160
although my crew has
taken quite a liking to it.

425
00:18:45.200 --> 00:18:46.920
I gotta get one of those things.

426
00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:49.010
Keep going!
Don't stop reading!

427
00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:51.550
Uh, we were told the
vessel had been manufactured

428
00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:52.840
on the sky island,

429
00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:55.450
and that many more interesting
devices could be found there.

430
00:18:55.810 --> 00:18:57.640
It brought to mind
an explorer friend of mine

431
00:18:57.680 --> 00:18:59.770
who once showed me
the most unusual specimen,

432
00:18:59.810 --> 00:19:01.650
a living sky fish.

433
00:19:01.660 --> 00:19:03.870
I have never seen a fish with
such a bizarre appearance

434
00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:05.700
– in all my years.
– Sky fish?

435
00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:08.370
This island remains
the only unexplored domain,

436
00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:10.920
and as a sailor and adventurer
I feel it is my destiny

437
00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:13.090
to one day reach
the sea in the sky.

438
00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:14.670
Mont Blanc Noland.

439
00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:16.010
Do you know what this means?

440
00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:17.930
The sky island
must really exist.

441
00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:19.850
Just like Robin said it did!

442
00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:22.270
Now we just gotta
figure out how to get there.

443
00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:24.610
The sky island does exist!

444
00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:28.440
You're up?

445
00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:29.690
You feeling all right?

446
00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:30.700
Better than ever.

447
00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:31.870
Glad to hear it.

448
00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:34.530
This jerk snuck up
on me and attacked me from...

449
00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:35.350
Shut up.

450
00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:36.760
I need to ask you something.

451
00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:38.160
What do you think of those guys?

452
00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:40.100
Why you askin', Old‐timer?

453
00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:41.900
It seems that they're
determined to get

454
00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:44.530
to sky island no matter what.

455
00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:46.000
Sky island?

456
00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:48.790
You know there's only
one way for them to get there.

457
00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:51.140
And if they try
to get there on their own,

458
00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:52.720
they'll die.

459
00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:54.180
Exactly.

460
00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:56.430
I think we should
help them along.

461
00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:58.730
So, what do you
guys think about that?
